WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:06.259
Happy Death Day was one of the surprise cinematic
successes of 2017, earning a nice mint at

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:11.320
the box office and entertaining audiences
with its Groundhog Day-meets-Scream plot line.

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:16.020
On the surface, the story is simple: a college
girl is forced to relive the same day over

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.039
and over until she finds out who's killing
her.

00:00:19.039 --> 00:00:24.150
But while the movie provides a fun mix of
genre humor and jump scares, Happy Death Day

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.180
also explores some deeper issues in character
development.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.960
Here's a look at what the ending to the film
really meant for our birthday girl.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:33.690
Mean girl redemption

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.930
In the beginning of the story, Tree is depicted
as the stereotypical sorority girl—basically

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:43.329
some snivelling version of Regina George and
Chanel from Scream Queens.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:47.270
She's mean and materialistic and doesn't seem
to care about anyone but herself.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:52.680
But while audiences are immediately inclined
to hate Tree, her backstory starts to trickle

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.780
in and offer some sympathetic elements along
the way.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:01:00.670
As the story moves forward, she becomes almost
likable and as the stabs roll in, they start

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.300
to sting more and more.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:06.720
Screenwriter Scott Lobdell told Thrillist
that he intended to subvert the traditional

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.170
heroine trope with Tree, saying:

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:13.290
"Every slasher film opens up with the mean
girl getting killed and the good girl living

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:14.290
till the end.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:17.890
And I thought, How can I make the mean girl
and the good girl the same person?"

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:22.869
There've been a lot of final girls to hit
the scream queen scene, but never quite like

00:01:22.869 --> 00:01:24.010
this.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:24.980
"I did it!

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:27.380
Wooooo!

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.140
Yeah!"

00:01:29.710 --> 00:01:31.580
A guardian angel?

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:36.270
One thing the film does not explain is the
scientific or supernatural reason for Tree's

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.270
time loop.

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:41.590
Director Christopher Landon has said that
he meant for it to be a big mystery, but that

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:44.690
hasn't stopped fans from guessing what started
it all.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:49.470
One popular theory is that her deceased mother
is orchestrating the whole thing from beyond

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:54.100
the grave to force Tree to finally deal with
her grief and reconcile with her father.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:58.880
After all, she continually leaves a bracelet
her mother gave her behind and seems to love

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.770
the night life's ability to drown out her
sorrows.

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:06.180
And she seems especially un-interested in
celebrating her birthday, which she just so

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.629
happens to share with her late mom.

00:02:08.629 --> 00:02:12.739
As she comes face to face with her mortality,
though, she starts to reflect on memories

00:02:12.739 --> 00:02:17.390
of her mom and eventually realizes that she
needs to help repair her fractured family

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:18.829
dynamic with her dad.

00:02:18.829 --> 00:02:27.040
"If my mom saw me, now -- who I've become
-- I don't think she'd be very proud."

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.930
The guardian angel concept might seem silly,
but how else do you explain a woman living

00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:34.939
the same bitter end again and again like this?

00:02:34.939 --> 00:02:37.019
A red herring

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:41.640
Since Tree's personal grim reaper is always
concealed by a baby mask, we don't know for

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.909
sure who's responsible for her many ends throughout
the movie.

00:02:44.909 --> 00:02:50.859
So, once we learn that a known serial slayer
named Joseph Tombs has been hospitalized nearby,

00:02:50.859 --> 00:02:53.540
he seems like just as good a suspect as any.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.709
Tree decides to get ahead of the attack and
stop Tombs in his tracks, but it takes a couple

00:02:57.709 --> 00:02:58.909
of tries to get it right.

00:02:58.909 --> 00:03:04.510
And even when she does manage to get the upper
hand on the vile villain, there's a catch.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.760
Since her budding beau Carter has tried to
save her and gets lost in the process, Tree

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.260
knows that if she breaks the time loop, she'll
sacrifice her new fella.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.499
As much as she might want out of the vicious
cycle, she still decides to off herself and

00:03:16.499 --> 00:03:17.829
try again instead.

00:03:17.829 --> 00:03:23.159
The scene showcases Tree's sudden sense of
selflessness and puts off the inevitable revelation

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.560
that Tombs isn't who she's really after anyway.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.080
Superficial killer, superficial world

00:03:29.449 --> 00:03:34.620
As we learn in the film's final moments, Tree's
attacker has been right under her nose the

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:35.879
entire time.

00:03:35.879 --> 00:03:41.499
Instead of an escaped convict or some stalker
ex, it's her jealous roommate Lori who arrives

00:03:41.499 --> 00:03:42.659
as the culprit.

00:03:42.659 --> 00:03:46.779
From the very beginning, Lori offers Tree
a birthday cupcake as a gift.

00:03:46.779 --> 00:03:49.370
And each time, our hero declines to eat it.

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:53.479
She finally takes a celebratory bite once
she believes she's vanquished her villain

00:03:53.479 --> 00:03:56.659
by taking out Jeffrey Tombs, and, whaddya
know.

00:03:56.659 --> 00:03:58.439
She wakes up again to the same thing.

00:03:58.439 --> 00:04:00.349
"This can't be happening.

00:04:00.349 --> 00:04:01.790
I killed him!

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.239
I stopped it!"

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:08.540
Tree then realizes that the cupcake caused
her demise and has one final fight with Lori.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.799
In the fracas, it's revealed that her roommate
started her slaughter spree because of jealousy

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:14.099
over a boy.

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:18.939
Tree manages to kick her roommate to the curb,
in a very literal sense, and finally manages

00:04:18.939 --> 00:04:23.470
to make it to tomorrow -- despite one cruel
morning prank pulled off by Carter.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:28.500
But more importantly, her true moment of salvation
stems from the fact that she's also laid waste

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:33.340
to some very bad habits and can start this
new leg of her life on the right foot.

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:34.340
Thanks for watching!

